WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.000 --> 00:00:02.467
-Our next guest can be seen

00:00:02.467 --> 00:00:06.133
at the Helium Comedy Club in
St. Louis November 2nd and 3rd.

00:00:06.133 --> 00:00:09.868
Please welcome
the very funny Josh Johnson.

00:00:09.868 --> 00:00:11.868
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:00:11.868 --> 00:00:25.801
♪♪

00:00:25.801 --> 00:00:27.501
-Cool.

00:00:27.501 --> 00:00:29.033
How y'all doing?
Everybody good?

00:00:29.033 --> 00:00:30.167
[ Audience cheers ]

00:00:30.167 --> 00:00:32.767
Okay.
I wish I could say the same.

00:00:32.767 --> 00:00:35.000
No, it's just I'm getting older
and I can feel it.

00:00:35.000 --> 00:00:36.267
This is rough.
This is --

00:00:36.267 --> 00:00:39.033
I've turned into, like,
an old black man but now.

00:00:39.033 --> 00:00:40.801
This is too soon.

00:00:40.801 --> 00:00:42.400
Two days after my birthday,

00:00:42.400 --> 00:00:44.834
somebody just said
something I agree with.

00:00:44.834 --> 00:00:46.601
Out of nowhere,
I was like, "Mm-hmm."

00:00:46.601 --> 00:00:49.501
That is unacceptable.

00:00:49.501 --> 00:00:51.167
I am 40 years
too young for that.

00:00:51.167 --> 00:00:54.801
A full civil rights "mm-hmm"
out of my throat.

00:00:54.801 --> 00:00:58.400
Every day I'm doing, like,
old-black-man things.

00:00:58.400 --> 00:01:02.200
I'll just see a cute couple,
start giving them advice.

00:01:02.200 --> 00:01:03.400
I was at a coffee shop.

00:01:03.400 --> 00:01:04.734
I saw two people
that looked good together.

00:01:04.734 --> 00:01:07.167
I was like, "Y'all take care
of each other now."

00:01:07.167 --> 00:01:09.534
Who is this for?

00:01:09.534 --> 00:01:12.200
I saw a little kid on a park
bench, shoulders hunched,

00:01:12.200 --> 00:01:13.567
looking like he didn't make
the team or something.

00:01:13.567 --> 00:01:14.601
I just ran up behind him.

00:01:14.601 --> 00:01:16.033
I was like,
"So you just gonna quit?"

00:01:16.033 --> 00:01:17.367
Like, this is --

00:01:17.367 --> 00:01:20.901
[ Laughter ]

00:01:20.901 --> 00:01:23.868
I'm starting to say sayings
that aren't real.

00:01:23.868 --> 00:01:26.300
My buddy had a bad day.
He was telling me about it.

00:01:26.300 --> 00:01:29.567
And when he got done,
I was like, "Well...

00:01:29.567 --> 00:01:32.100
bottom of the shoe ain't nothing
without the top of the foot."

00:01:32.100 --> 00:01:34.534
Like...
[ Laughter ]

00:01:34.534 --> 00:01:35.734
I don't even know
what that means.

00:01:35.734 --> 00:01:37.801
I'm just saying things.

00:01:37.801 --> 00:01:41.534
You know, and my friends
have been dating,

00:01:41.534 --> 00:01:43.868
and I've watched them
as they date.

00:01:43.868 --> 00:01:48.067
And I've noticed a lot of what
people call red flags, right?

00:01:48.067 --> 00:01:51.133
And people have to
look out for red flags early.

00:01:51.133 --> 00:01:56.000
Like, ladies, you know red flags
that we don't even register.

00:01:56.000 --> 00:01:58.534
You really do.
I was at a club with my friend.

00:01:58.534 --> 00:02:00.300
He had a real
rom-com experience.

00:02:00.300 --> 00:02:02.534
He saw this woman
across the club and he said --

00:02:02.534 --> 00:02:04.467
He was like, "The light
was just shining on her

00:02:04.467 --> 00:02:06.267
a little extra," you know?

00:02:06.267 --> 00:02:07.801
And so he made his way
towards her,

00:02:07.801 --> 00:02:09.801
and the dance floor was parting.

00:02:09.801 --> 00:02:11.234
People were getting
out of the way

00:02:11.234 --> 00:02:13.434
as he was walking up to her,
and he walks right up to her.

00:02:13.434 --> 00:02:17.767
He lays his thickest game.
He's like, "Hey, how you doing?

00:02:17.767 --> 00:02:19.701
Maybe I'll buy you
a drink or something."

00:02:19.701 --> 00:02:22.467
She turned around
and she looked him up and down.

00:02:22.467 --> 00:02:25.767
She was like, "Oh, uh, no."

00:02:25.767 --> 00:02:27.634
[ Laughter ]

00:02:27.634 --> 00:02:30.200
"No, it's not for me.
That's not what we do."

00:02:30.200 --> 00:02:31.801
And he tried one more time.

00:02:31.801 --> 00:02:34.033
He was like, "Why can't I buy
you a drink, get to know you?

00:02:34.033 --> 00:02:36.000
Maybe we might like each other."

00:02:36.000 --> 00:02:39.167
And she looked him up
and down again and she said,

00:02:39.167 --> 00:02:41.601
"Your shoes."

00:02:41.601 --> 00:02:43.634
I know that sounds super rude.

00:02:43.634 --> 00:02:46.200
It sounds vapid, materialistic,
but I'm not going to lie.

00:02:46.200 --> 00:02:50.200
I'm on her side,
because when he looked down,

00:02:50.200 --> 00:02:52.367
both his shoes were untied,
and it's like,

00:02:52.367 --> 00:02:55.200
yeah, that's a red flag.

00:02:55.200 --> 00:02:57.334
There's no better way to walk up
to a woman and be like,

00:02:57.334 --> 00:02:58.734
"You gonna have to take care
of me eventually,"

00:02:58.734 --> 00:03:01.601
than to walk up
with both your shoes untied.

00:03:01.601 --> 00:03:02.601
[ Laughter ]

00:03:02.601 --> 00:03:04.634
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:03:08.367 --> 00:03:11.734
How'd he even make it across the
dance floor without tripping?

00:03:11.734 --> 00:03:13.200
And I'm trying to be fair here.

00:03:13.200 --> 00:03:14.901
One shoe could
happen to anybody.

00:03:14.901 --> 00:03:16.334
We're all human.

00:03:16.334 --> 00:03:20.067
Both shoes have not happened
since the third grade.

00:03:20.067 --> 00:03:22.601
If you approach a woman
with both your shoes untied,

00:03:22.601 --> 00:03:25.567
you might as well walk up
with a glass of milk in one hand

00:03:25.567 --> 00:03:27.167
and a cookie in the other

00:03:27.167 --> 00:03:30.133
talking about,
"You want to be friends?"

00:03:30.133 --> 00:03:31.934
'Cause that's what you look
and sound like

00:03:31.934 --> 00:03:34.934
when both your shoes are untied.

00:03:34.934 --> 00:03:37.033
I'm very blessed to be here,
by the way,

00:03:37.033 --> 00:03:39.534
I -- This is absolutely amazing.

00:03:39.534 --> 00:03:40.534
I'm --

00:03:40.534 --> 00:03:42.534
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:03:45.334 --> 00:03:47.334
I grew up in Louisiana

00:03:47.334 --> 00:03:50.834
and I feel like
I had an incredible childhood,

00:03:50.834 --> 00:03:52.901
even though I don't come
from money, you know?

00:03:52.901 --> 00:03:54.868
And I was having a conversation
with my friend.

00:03:54.868 --> 00:03:56.300
He gave me
a really interesting metric.

00:03:56.300 --> 00:03:59.734
He said, "You can tell how good
someone's childhood was

00:03:59.734 --> 00:04:03.634
in the States by how long
they believed in Santa."

00:04:03.634 --> 00:04:05.534
[ Laughter ]

00:04:05.534 --> 00:04:08.067
If they were told about Santa
and they believed in Santa,

00:04:08.067 --> 00:04:10.100
how long they believed
is how coddled,

00:04:10.100 --> 00:04:11.701
you know what I mean,
just how sheltered they were,

00:04:11.701 --> 00:04:14.901
because we all remember
that kid...

00:04:14.901 --> 00:04:16.734
that was like 15.

00:04:16.734 --> 00:04:17.734
[ Laughter ]

00:04:17.734 --> 00:04:18.934
And Christmas comes around.

00:04:18.934 --> 00:04:20.067
He's like,
"I can't wait for Santa."

00:04:20.067 --> 00:04:22.267
You're like, "Oh, no.

00:04:22.267 --> 00:04:25.934
Oh, they too good to him.
This is terrible."

00:04:25.934 --> 00:04:27.834
I had an incredible childhood,
but I'm not going to lie.

00:04:27.834 --> 00:04:30.100
I stopped believing in Santa
pretty young,

00:04:30.100 --> 00:04:32.133
because when you don't have
a good money situation,

00:04:32.133 --> 00:04:33.868
you usually don't have
a good living situation.

00:04:33.868 --> 00:04:36.334
So my neighborhood
was really rough.

00:04:36.334 --> 00:04:39.033
And when I was 8 years old,

00:04:39.033 --> 00:04:40.501
I saw Santa get robbed.

00:04:40.501 --> 00:04:41.667
[ Laughter ]

00:04:41.667 --> 00:04:43.067
And I started asking questions.

00:04:43.067 --> 00:04:44.534
I was like, "How does he
make to every house

00:04:44.534 --> 00:04:45.834
in the entire world in one night

00:04:45.834 --> 00:04:47.334
but he can't get away
from these dudes?

00:04:47.334 --> 00:04:49.067
'Cause he is getting whupped."

00:04:49.067 --> 00:04:51.167
[ Laughter ]

00:04:57.267 --> 00:05:01.167
I had to call 911
at the beginning of this year.

00:05:01.167 --> 00:05:04.334
And I appreciate just about
everything that these people do.

00:05:04.334 --> 00:05:06.701
They are literally heroes,
you know?

00:05:06.701 --> 00:05:08.634
The only thing
I don't appreciate

00:05:08.634 --> 00:05:11.100
is how calm they are
when they pick up.

00:05:11.100 --> 00:05:14.133
That feels disrespectful
to my situation,

00:05:14.133 --> 00:05:15.601
'cause I'm not calling to chat.

00:05:15.601 --> 00:05:17.434
Something traumatic
just happened in front of me.

00:05:17.434 --> 00:05:20.300
And I call, and they pick up,
and they're like, "911.

00:05:20.300 --> 00:05:22.200
What's your emergency?"

00:05:22.200 --> 00:05:24.434
It's like, "No, my tax dollars
cannot be going

00:05:24.434 --> 00:05:27.434
to that department store
customer service level energy."

00:05:27.434 --> 00:05:30.801
Alright? I'm freaking out.
I need you freaking out, okay?

00:05:30.801 --> 00:05:33.701
It would feel better to call 911
and be like, "Oh, my goodness!"

00:05:33.701 --> 00:05:34.868
And they're like, "What is it?!"

00:05:34.868 --> 00:05:37.334
That would feel so much better.

00:05:37.334 --> 00:05:39.901
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:05:45.033 --> 00:05:46.801
Because don't get me wrong,
I get it.

00:05:46.801 --> 00:05:49.200
They're calm so that I'm calm,

00:05:49.200 --> 00:05:51.067
because if I'm calm,
I'm a better communicator

00:05:51.067 --> 00:05:53.434
and they can more effectively
help me with my situation.

00:05:53.434 --> 00:05:56.868
I understand why they do it.
It just doesn't feel good.

00:05:56.868 --> 00:05:58.834
'Cause then you tell them
everything that's going wrong

00:05:58.834 --> 00:06:01.467
and then they repeat
your emergency back to you

00:06:01.467 --> 00:06:03.601
like a fast-food order.

00:06:03.601 --> 00:06:06.968
They're on the phone like,
"So you telling me...

00:06:10.000 --> 00:06:15.601
...that your friend fell out
and now he on the ground

00:06:15.601 --> 00:06:19.234
and you don't know
if he breathing.

00:06:19.234 --> 00:06:23.000
And so you put a spoon
in front of his face

00:06:23.000 --> 00:06:26.200
and the spoon fogged up,
so he might be breathing.

00:06:28.934 --> 00:06:30.667
But that might also be
your breath

00:06:30.667 --> 00:06:32.901
'cause you were too close
to the spoon."

00:06:32.901 --> 00:06:36.834
I'm like,
"Just send somebody, please."

00:06:36.834 --> 00:06:39.734
And nobody talks about it,
but if 911 doesn't believe you,

00:06:39.734 --> 00:06:42.267
they can just hang up.

00:06:42.267 --> 00:06:44.200
That's a real thing.
If they don't take you serious,

00:06:44.200 --> 00:06:45.667
they think it's a prank,
they can just hang up.

00:06:45.667 --> 00:06:48.234
And I know that because
when I was 8 years old,

00:06:48.234 --> 00:06:52.968
I called 911 to tell them
that Santa was getting whupped.

00:06:52.968 --> 00:06:54.033
[ Laughter ]

00:06:54.033 --> 00:06:56.133
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:07:00.934 --> 00:07:03.400
And they hung up in my face.

00:07:03.400 --> 00:07:06.934
They were like, "So you're
telling me that Santa --

00:07:06.934 --> 00:07:08.968
Sir, we have real things
to do here."

00:07:08.968 --> 00:07:10.601
I'm Josh Johnson.
Thanks so much.

00:07:10.601 --> 00:07:11.868
Y'all have a great night.

00:07:11.868 --> 00:07:13.968
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:07:13.968 --> 00:07:18.501
♪♪

00:07:18.501 --> 00:07:21.200
-Josh Johnson.

00:07:21.200 --> 00:07:24.300
See him at
the Helium Comedy Club

00:07:24.300 --> 00:07:26.701
in St. Louis
November 2nd and 3rd.

00:07:26.701 --> 00:07:29.133
We'll be right back, everybody.
Come on back.

